= Multi-agent system — Technical =

== Agent set ==
A multi-agent system can be represented abstractly by agents:

<pre>
A = {a_1, ..., a_n}
</pre>

with local observations, policies, action spaces, and interaction mechanisms.

Each agent's outcome can depend on both its own action and the actions/messages of others.

== Interaction structures ==
MAS models can include:

* cooperative distributed control;
* stochastic games;
* decentralized partially observable decision processes;
* auctions and mechanism design;
* coalition formation;
* distributed constraint optimization;
* communication protocols.

No single formalism covers the entire field.

== Centralization ==
A system can have centralized training, centralized coordination, decentralized execution, or fully decentralized control.

“Multi-agent” does not imply absence of an orchestrator.


== Formal interaction models ==
A stochastic game specifies a state, joint action, transition law and reward for each agent. In a cooperative decentralized partially observable decision process, agents share a team objective but may observe different information and select actions from their local histories. Shared rewards do not eliminate coordination difficulties: distinct agents may have incomplete information about each other's state, and joint action spaces grow as the product of individual action-set sizes. Competitive systems need separate incentive and equilibrium analyses. A message protocol may coordinate policy execution without making all internal states common knowledge.

== Coordination and topology ==
Communication graphs specify who can exchange messages, while task dependencies specify which results are required by other tasks. These relations are not interchangeable. For a fixed connected undirected graph, local averaging may reach consensus under appropriate weights; changing topology, delays and faults alter the guarantees. Agreement may still be wrong if every agent shares a mistaken premise. Mechanisms for delegation should specify ownership, dependencies, completion conditions and validation rather than infer work from messages claiming completion.

== Evaluation and system safety ==
Use task-matched single-agent, independent-agent and coordinated-agent baselines with equal resource budgets. Record success, cost, latency, redundant work, communication, correlated failures and recovery under controlled perturbations. Test contradictory messages and unavailable dependencies in a bounded environment. Centralized orchestration may improve accountability but can create a bottleneck; decentralized control may improve fault tolerance but complicate verification. No topology alone establishes reliable or safe collective behavior.

